const LABEL_IN_NAME_SWEEP = `(() => {
  // WCAG 2.5.3 "Label in Name": if a control's accessible name comes from
  // aria-label/aria-labelledby while it also shows text, that visible text must
  // still be part of the name — a speech user says what they see. Axe-core
  // normalises both sides the same way this does (lower-case, parentheticals
  // dropped, non-alphanumerics as separators, contiguous token run).
  const tokens = (text) => String(text).toLowerCase()
    .replace(/\\([^()]*\\)/g, ' ')
    .replace(/[^\\p{L}\\p{N}]+/gu, ' ')
    .trim().split(/\\s+/).filter(Boolean);
  const contains = (name, label) => {
    const n = tokens(name), l = tokens(label);
    if (!l.length) return true;
    for (let i = 0; i + l.length <= n.length; i++) if (l.every((t, j) => t === n[i + j])) return true;
    return false;
  };
  const visibleText = (node) => { ... };
  ...
})()`
```

For visible text: axe uses `visible_virtual` with pixel thresholds — I can approximate: `node.innerText` (rendered text, excludes hidden children, includes text-transform). But for elements whose visible text is only an icon (e.g. "+" button), `isHumanInterpretable` matters: axe returns `undefined` (inapplicable) if the label has no letters/digits. My check should skip when the visible text has no letter/number tokens — e.g. '+' → tokens [] → `contains` returns true (I wrote `if (!l.length) return true`). So `+`/`−` buttons pass automatically. 

For the accessible name: if `aria-labelledby` exists, compute referenced elements' innerText; else `aria-label`; else skip (name from content = visible text by construction). Also skip `hidden`/invisible elements (`offsetParent === null` / `getClientRects().length === 0`), and skip elements with `role="presentation"`? Not needed.

